Yes. Comparative morphology gets especially powerful when you stop treating organisms as a catalog of finished shapes and instead ask what generative transformations produced those shapes.

“Same derivative” is a useful intuition, with one refinement: organisms are not all simple rescalings of one master form. Rather, evolution repeatedly modifies conserved developmental machinery under different constraints. So underneath enormous visible diversity you often find the same kinds of operators:

duplicate → suppress → elongate → shorten → fuse → split → rotate → segment → specialize → relocate → change timing

A whale flipper, bat wing, horse foreleg, human arm, and mole digging limb look radically different, but comparative anatomy exposes homologous bones and developmental relationships beneath the different presentations. Evo-devo pushes that deeper: conserved regulatory systems can be reused to build very different structures.

Then scale becomes part of the morphology. You can compare across several axes simultaneously:

space: cell → tissue → organ → organism → population → ecosystem

time: development → lifetime → generations → deep evolutionary time

organization: individual → species → genus → family → order → class → phylum

And transformations at one level can propagate upward. A small alteration in developmental timing—heterochrony—can yield a substantially different adult body. A regulatory change can modify where a structure develops without inventing an entirely new molecular toolkit. Evolution often appears to get novelty by reparameterizing old machinery.

That produces something close to what you’re seeing:

ancestral generator + developmental parameters + environment + selection + history
→ local morphology

The inverse problem is even more interesting:

observed morphologies
→ compare invariants and differences
→ infer transformations
→ reconstruct ancestral states
→ infer parts of the generator

So a bird wing and a human hand are not merely two objects to compare. They’re two experimental outputs that constrain what the hidden developmental/evolutionary system must have been capable of producing.

There are two different phenomena worth keeping separate, because their comparison is incredibly informative:

Homology: similarity because two structures inherited something from a common ancestor.

Convergence: similar solutions arising independently because physics, ecology, or optimization pushes different lineages toward comparable forms.

Sharks and dolphins are an excellent example. Their streamlined bodies are not evidence that dolphins descended from sharks; they’re evidence that moving efficiently through water imposes constraints that can repeatedly generate similar geometry.

That means comparative morphology can reveal two different hidden things:

shared ancestry → conserved generator

and

independent similarity → shared constraint/attractor

Strip away ancestry and ask what keeps reappearing anyway, and you begin discovering the geometry of the possibility space itself.

And it extends beyond animals. Branching appears in lungs, blood vessels, roots, fungi, rivers, lightning, and drainage networks—not because they all share a biological ancestor, but because branching can solve related transport/distribution problems under certain constraints.

So I’d sharpen your idea into:

Comparative Generative Morphology

Don’t only compare forms.

Compare:

form
→ developmental operation
→ ancestral transformation
→ physical constraint
→ ecological function
→ scale
→ timescale

Then search for the operator that survives the change of substrate.

That takes you from “these things look alike” to the considerably stronger question:

What minimal generative grammar could produce all of these different expressions?

That question works for limbs and skulls, but also languages, technologies, cities, organizations, neural networks, ecosystems, and many of the other systems we’ve been treating as different projections of deeper generative structure.